-- More Reading practice tests for the practice test library: two published PRACTICE_TEST packages (IELTS_SPACE_PRO and
-- C10_C20), three passages of eight questions each, so every Passage tab and Full đề has several tests to choose from.
-- Passages and questions are original text written for this project. Every question has purpose EXAM and a CHOICE or FILL
-- answer spec. Cover images are small inline SVG data URIs. Every statement is idempotent.


B. The animals have adapted quickly to life among people. They shelter under garden sheds, cross busy roads at night and feed on a wide range of food, from earthworms to scraps left in rubbish bins. Biologists say this flexible diet is the main reason they thrive where other mammals have declined.

C. Residents are divided about their new neighbours. Some enjoy watching cubs play on the lawn, while others complain of noise during the mating season and of overturned bins. Several councils now publish leaflets advising householders how to keep food waste out of reach.

D. Researchers have recently begun to fit urban foxes with lightweight collars. The data show that a typical animal covers less than two kilometres in a night, far less than its countryside cousins, because food is plentiful and close at hand.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000102', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000102', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Coral reefs shelter a quarter of all marine species, yet in the last thirty years many have lost half of their living coral to warming seas and pollution. In response, marine scientists have started to grow coral in underwater nurseries and then transplant it onto damaged reefs.

B. The method is simple in principle. Small fragments are cut from healthy colonies and attached to frames made from plastic pipe or rope. Because fragments grow far faster than coral spawned naturally, a nursery can produce thousands of new colonies in a single year.

C. Nursery gardeners must choose their fragments carefully. Colonies that survived a recent heatwave are favoured, since they may carry some tolerance to high temperatures that can be passed on to their offspring.

D. Critics argue that nurseries treat the symptoms rather than the cause. Even the healthiest transplanted coral will bleach again if oceans keep warming. Supporters accept this but say restoration buys time while governments cut emissions.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000103', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000103', 'PASSAGE', 'A. For a century, ferries have run on heavy diesel, and a single large vessel can burn as much fuel in a year as thousands of cars. The first fully electric passenger ferry, launched on a Norwegian fjord in 2015, was therefore greeted as a small revolution.

B. Its batteries are charged in about ten minutes at each end of the crossing, and the vessel uses roughly a tenth of the energy of its diesel predecessor. Passengers notice little except the quiet, as there is no engine hum and no exhaust smell on the deck.

C. Cost remains the main obstacle. A battery ferry is more expensive to build, and short routes with frequent stops suit it far better than long ones. Operators on longer crossings are therefore testing hybrid designs that combine batteries with a small engine.

D. Port authorities have a part to play. Charging a large ferry demands a powerful connection to the grid, and many harbours do not have one. Several governments now offer grants to help ports install the equipment, and analysts expect orders to rise over the next decade.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000104', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000104', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Many people assume that libraries are fading away, yet in several countries visitor numbers have risen for a decade. Librarians explain that the buildings are no longer used only for borrowing books; they have become places to study, to meet and to learn new skills.

B. Modern libraries often contain quiet study rooms, free computer stations and rooms where residents can attend language classes. In one northern city, a library even lends out tools such as drills and ladders, which has proved especially popular with young families.

C. Funding remains a concern. Councils facing tight budgets have closed small branches, and campaigners argue that these closures hit older people and those without internet access hardest. Some communities have responded by running local libraries with volunteers.

D. Surveys show that people value libraries most as free and neutral spaces. Visitors say they feel welcome even if they do not spend any money, something that is increasingly rare in town centres.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000105', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000105', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Teenagers are often accused of laziness because they stay up late and struggle to wake for school. Sleep researchers argue that this pattern is largely biological: during adolescence the body clock shifts, and the hormone that signals bedtime is released about two hours later than in children.

B. A school in Seattle tested the idea by moving its start time from 7.50 to 8.45 a.m. Within a year, students were sleeping on average 34 minutes longer each night, and attendance and grades both improved.

C. Not every expert is persuaded. Some point out that later starts push the end of the school day into the evening, which may disrupt sport and part-time jobs. Others say that families must also help by limiting screen use before bed.

D. Most agree that more research is needed. Large studies currently under way will follow thousands of students for several years to see whether the gains seen in individual schools can be repeated elsewhere.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000106', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2d000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000106', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Britain has lost around two thirds of its traditional orchards since 1950, replaced by housing, supermarkets'' preference for a few uniform varieties, and cheap imported fruit. Yet each surviving orchard is a refuge for wildlife, from nesting birds to rare beetles that live only in decaying wood.

B. Community groups are now bringing neglected orchards back to life. Volunteers prune overgrown trees, graft cuttings from old varieties onto new roots and hold autumn festivals where visitors can taste apples that are almost never sold in shops.

C. The work is not only about fruit. Orchard sites often hold meadows beneath the trees, and these grasslands support bees and butterflies. Ecologists say that cutting the grass only once a year, after wildflowers have set seed, benefits the greatest number of species.

D. Restoration has its difficulties. Old trees are slow to recover, and a newly planted tree may not bear a useful crop for seven years. Even so, organisers say the sight of blossom in spring is reward enough for many volunteers.', NULL, 'VALID')
